    'intro'  => '<p>New Orleans has a distinct rental market shaped by universities, a large medical community and a busy events calendar. Tulane and Loyola students, medical residents, travel nurses, film and production crews and remote workers all look for furnished, month-to-month housing.</p><p><strong>Uptown</strong> and the <strong>Garden District</strong> are known for shotgun houses and grand homes along the St. Charles streetcar line, the <strong>Marigny</strong> and <strong>Bywater</strong> downriver offer colorful Creole cottages and live music, and <strong>Mid-City</strong> is a residential area near City Park. Many rentals are units in older shotgun doubles or converted homes.</p><p>The city regulates short-term rentals strictly, so confirm that a stay is properly set up as a monthly rental. Hurricane season and summer heat are also worth planning around. Conditions change block by block, so research the specific street, ask about flooding and parking, and check how close the nearest streetcar or bus line runs.</p>',
