    'intro'  => '<p>Hartford is Connecticut&rsquo;s capital and long known as the Insurance Capital of the World, with several major insurers headquartered or operating large offices here. That corporate base, plus state government, hospitals and colleges, creates demand for furnished housing from interns, legislative staff, travel nurses and relocating employees.</p><p>Within the city, the <strong>West End</strong> is a historic neighborhood of large Victorian homes near Elizabeth Park, <strong>Downtown</strong> has converted office buildings and apartments, and <strong>Asylum Hill</strong> is close to several insurance offices. Many renters also look just outside the city in <strong>West Hartford</strong>, with its walkable center and Blue Back Square.</p><p>Hartford sits between New York and Boston, and the Hartford Line commuter rail connects it to New Haven and Springfield, making it a practical base for regional travel. Living costs are generally lower than in Boston or New York, which appeals to renters who travel around the region for work.</p>',
    'neighborhoods' => [
      ['name' => 'West End', 'blurb' => 'Historic Victorian homes and apartments near Elizabeth Park and the University of Saint Joseph.'],
      ['name' => 'Downtown', 'blurb' => 'Converted office buildings and newer apartments near Union Station and the State Capitol.'],
      ['name' => 'Asylum Hill', 'blurb' => 'Close to several insurance offices and the Mark Twain House.'],
      ['name' => 'Parkville', 'blurb' => 'A neighborhood west of downtown with an arts scene and lower rents.'],
      ['name' => 'West Hartford Center', 'blurb' => 'A walkable suburban downtown with restaurants and shops.'],
      ['name' => 'Blue Back Square', 'blurb' => 'A mixed-use area in West Hartford with apartments, shops and dining.'],
    ],
    'prices' => '<p>Furnished private rooms in the Hartford area commonly advertise somewhere around $700&ndash;$1,100 per month, and entire furnished one-bedrooms typically run higher, often $1,300&ndash;$2,100 depending on location.</p><p>West Hartford tends to cost more than the city. Prices shift with the season, location and furnishings, so check current listings before you decide.</p>',
    'transit'=> '<p>CTtransit runs local buses, and CTfastrak bus rapid transit connects downtown Hartford with New Britain. Union Station is served by the Hartford Line commuter rail and Amtrak. Most residents drive.</p><p>Bradley International Airport (BDL) in Windsor Locks is about 20 minutes north of downtown by car.</p>',
